I cleaned up your README into practical notes, separating without Docker, with Docker, database verification, troubleshooting, and Docker Hub. I kept the commands and project terminology from your README.

FastAPI + MySQL — Run & Docker Notes
1. Project setup — WITHOUT Docker
Create virtual environment
python3 -m venv myvenv

Activate:

source myvenv/bin/activate

Install dependencies:

pip install -r requirements.txt
Run FastAPI

Depending on which app you want:

uvicorn main:app --reload

or:

uvicorn project:app --reload

or authentication app:

uvicorn auth.main:app --reload

Your API docs:

http://localhost:8000/docs

The README also contains uvicorn crud:app --reload.

2. MySQL — WITHOUT Docker
Check MySQL installation
brew list | grep mysql
mysql --version

If not installed:

brew install mysql

Start MySQL:

brew services start mysql

Check:

brew services list | grep mysql

Expected:

mysql started

The README notes that an error such as Can't connect to local MySQL server through socket means MySQL isn't running; the password isn't the first issue to troubleshoot.

3. Connect to MySQL
mysql -u root -p

If your root password is empty, press Enter when prompted.

Create database:

CREATE DATABASE fastapi_crud;

Check:

SHOW DATABASES;

Select it:

USE fastapi_crud;

Create tables from Python:

python create_table.py

Then verify:

SHOW TABLES;

Run FastAPI:

uvicorn project:app --reload

These are the README's original non-Docker database steps.

4. Check database data

Inside MySQL:

USE fastapi_crud;

Check tables:

SHOW TABLES;

Check books:

SELECT * FROM books;

Check structure:

DESCRIBE books;

Specific columns:

SELECT id, title, author FROM books;

Count records:

SELECT COUNT(*) FROM books;

5. Authentication — WITHOUT Docker

Go to auth:

cd auth

Generate secret key:

python key.py

Then run:

uvicorn main:app --reload

Create auth table:

python create_auth_table.py

Or, from the project root:

python -m auth.create_auth_table

Run auth API from project root:

uvicorn auth.main:app --reload

6. Freeze Python dependencies

After installing everything:

pip freeze > requirements.txt

Your README lists dependencies including:

fastapi
uvicorn
sqlalchemy
pymysql
pydantic
requests
passlib[argon2]
python-jose[cryptography]
python-multipart
pydantic[email]

7. Docker — Project structure

For multiple containers:

FastAPI container
       ↓
MySQL container

Use:

docker-compose.yml

The Compose file orchestrates the containers.

8. Docker — Important files

You need:

FASTAPICRUD/
│
├── Dockerfile
├── .dockerignore
├── docker-compose.yml
├── requirements.txt
├── auth/
├── main.py
└── ...
.dockerignore
myvenv/
__pycache__/
.git/
.env
.DS_Store
Dockerfile
FROM python:3.11-slim

WORKDIR /app

COPY requirements.txt .

RUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt

COPY . .

EXPOSE 8000

CMD ["uvicorn", "auth.main:app", "--host", "0.0.0.0", "--port", "8000"]
9. Docker — Validate Compose

First:

docker compose config

This checks whether your Compose YAML is valid.

Check services:

docker compose config --services

Expected:

db
fastapi

The README specifically uses these commands to validate the Compose configuration before starting containers.

10. Docker — Build & start

Build and start:

docker compose up --build -d

--build → build image
-d → run in background

Check:

docker compose ps

Expected:

mysql_container   Up (healthy)
fastapi_app       Up

Your current setup uses MySQL internally on:

db:3306

and FastAPI on:

localhost:8000
11. Docker — Open API

Browser:

http://localhost:8000/docs

This shows your FastAPI Swagger/OpenAPI endpoints

12. Docker — Create auth table

Enter FastAPI container:

docker exec -it fastapi_app bash

Check location:

pwd

Expected:

/app

Check files:

ls

Then create table:

python -m auth.create_auth_table

Important: Docker commands such as docker compose are run from your Mac terminal, not inside fastapi_app.

13. Docker — Verify MySQL tables

From your Mac terminal:

docker exec -it mysql_container mysql -u root -p

For your current Docker setup, password is empty → press Enter.

Then:

SHOW DATABASES;

Select:

USE fastapi_db;

Check tables:

SHOW TABLES;

Check users:

DESCRIBE users;

Check data:

SELECT * FROM users;

Your current Docker database is fastapi_db, while your older non-Docker setup uses fastapi_crud.

14. Docker troubleshooting cheat sheet
Validate YAML
docker compose config
See services
docker compose config --services
See containers, including stopped
docker compose ps -a
FastAPI logs
docker compose logs fastapi
MySQL logs
docker compose logs db
Build + start with visible logs
docker compose up --build
Build + start in background
docker compose up --build -d
Stop/remove containers
docker compose down
Remove containers + volumes
docker compose down -v

⚠️ -v removes the Compose database volume, so database data can be lost.

Build only
docker compose build
Execute command inside FastAPI
docker compose exec fastapi bash

The README's debugging sequence is essentially: Compose → YAML → services → containers → image/Dockerfile → startup → logs → application error.

15. Docker Hub — Push image

Login:

docker login

Tag:

docker tag fastapicrud-fastapi nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql:latest

Push:

docker push nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql

The README notes that pushing the image publicly allows another system to pull and use it.

16. Docker Hub — Pull image on another machine
docker pull nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql:latest

Then Docker can use that image instead of rebuilding it locally.

⭐ Quick command sheet
WITHOUT Docker
cd FASTAPICRUD

python3 -m venv myvenv
source myvenv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt

brew services start mysql

uvicorn auth.main:app --reload
WITH Docker
cd FASTAPICRUD

docker compose config
docker compose config --services

docker compose up --build -d

docker compose ps

Then:

http://localhost:8000/docs

Create auth table:

docker exec -it fastapi_app bash
python -m auth.create_auth_table

Verify MySQL:

docker exec -it mysql_container mysql -u root -p
USE fastapi_db;
SHOW TABLES;
SELECT * FROM users;
If something breaks
docker compose ps -a
docker compose logs fastapi
docker compose logs db
Fresh Docker restart
docker compose down -v
docker compose up --build -d

This is the clean sequence I would keep as your main cheat sheet; the longer sections are for troubleshooting/reference.


Yes. If you push your FastAPI image to Docker Hub, another user can pull the same image and run it on their machine.

1. Build your image

From your project root:

cd /Users/nidhikumari/Desktop/FASTAPICRUD

docker compose build

Check the image name:

docker images

You should see something like:

fastapicrud-fastapi
2. Login to Docker Hub
docker login

Enter your Docker Hub username/password.

3. Tag your image

For example, if your Docker Hub username is nidhiksah:

docker tag fastapicrud-fastapi nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql:latest

Verify:

docker images

You should now have:

nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql   latest
4. Push it
docker push nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql:latest

Now the image is available on Docker Hub.

5. Another user can download it

They don't need your source code or Dockerfile.

They simply run:

docker pull nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql:latest

Then:

docker run -p 8000:8000 nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql:latest

But ⚠️ your FastAPI image depends on MySQL, so simply running the FastAPI image isn't enough. The other user also needs a MySQL container and the correct network/environment configuration.

For your project, the better approach is to publish the FastAPI image to Docker Hub + provide the docker-compose.yml. Then another user can do:

docker compose up -d

and get:

FastAPI container
        +
MySQL container

using your already-built FastAPI image.


Yes. For your current project, I’d set it up so Docker Hub provides the FastAPI image, while Docker Compose creates the MySQL container and connects both automatically.

1. Push your current FastAPI image

From FASTAPICRUD:

docker compose build
docker tag fastapicrud-fastapi nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql:latest
docker push nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql:latest
2. Change docker-compose.yml

For distribution, use the Docker Hub image instead of build: .:

services:
  db:
    image: mysql:8.0
    container_name: mysql_container
    environment:
      MYSQL_ALLOW_EMPTY_PASSWORD: "yes"
      MYSQL_DATABASE: fastapi_db
    volumes:
      - mysql_data:/var/lib/mysql
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD", "mysqladmin", "ping", "-h", "localhost"]
      interval: 10s
      timeout: 5s
      retries: 5

  fastapi:
    image: nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql:latest
    container_name: fastapi_app
    depends_on:
      db:
        condition: service_healthy
    ports:
      - "8000:8000"
    environment:
      MYSQL_HOST: db
      MYSQL_PORT: 3306
      MYSQL_DATABASE: fastapi_db
      MYSQL_USER: root
      MYSQL_PASSWORD: ""

volumes:
  mysql_data:

Important: Remove:

build: .

because other users don't need to build your FastAPI image.

3. Test exactly like another user

First stop your current setup:

docker compose down -v

Then:

docker compose up -d

Compose will automatically:

Pull nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql:latest
Pull mysql:8.0 if needed
Create the network
Start MySQL
Wait until MySQL is healthy
Start FastAPI

Check:

docker compose ps

Then open:

http://localhost:8000/docs
4. Create the users table

Because your image contains auth/create_auth_table.py:

docker exec -it fastapi_app bash

Inside the container:

python -m auth.create_auth_table

Exit:

exit
5. What you give another developer

Your GitHub/project package only needs something like:

FASTAPICRUD/
├── docker-compose.yml
└── README.md

They do not need your Python source code to run the published FastAPI image.

Their process becomes:

git clone <your-project>
cd FASTAPICRUD
docker compose up -d

Then:

http://localhost:8000/docs
One important improvement

I recommend not using latest for production/distribution. Tag versions:

docker tag fastapicrud-fastapi nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql:1.0.0
docker push nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql:1.0.0

Then Compose uses:

image: nidhiksah/fastapi_mysql:1.0.0

That way, if you release 1.1.0, existing users continue using 1.0.0 instead of unexpectedly getting a new image.















